# THE NAMEPLATE AMOUNTS WHOSE UNIT CARRIES A PERIOD. One row per layer whose supply is quoted
# as a RATE -- `4 GPU-hours per week` -- as against a STOCK, `12 people`.
#
# ⭐⭐ WHY THE DISTINCTION IS A RELATION AND NOT AN `IF`. "What fraction of the period is this
#    supply available?" is a malformed question against a stock: `12 people` has no period, so
#    "five days" has nothing to be five days of. Against a rate it is perfectly answerable, and
#    `notApplicable` there is a refusal dressed as a category error. The set of units that have
#    a period is therefore the thing that decides whether an absence is honest.
#
# ✅⭐⭐⭐ IT WAS CALLED `with_denominator` AND IT TESTED THE WRONG PREDICATE. `LIKE '% per %' OR
#    LIKE '% por %'` finds a denominator; this rule needs a PERIOD, and the corpus separates
#    them -- `asrt:factor` files `GPU-hour per GPU`, whose denominator is a GPU. At nameplate
#    grain the two had never disagreed, so the file could carry the wrong name and the right
#    rows for as long as nobody filed the case that splits them.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND THE TWO EDITIONS ARE GONE FROM THE SQL ENTIRELY. `shifts per week` and `turnos por
#    semana` both file `pm:denominator/pm:period`, so nothing here decides that `semana` IS
#    `week`. That judgement used to live in an `OR` and belonged to no filing.
-- pm:Claim/pm:denominator/pm:period, at pm:Nameplate/amount.
SELECT c.filing, c.layer, c.unit, c.denominator AS period
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/claims.sqlc)
) c
WHERE c.owns = 'pm:amount'
  AND c.denominator_kind = 'period'
